// Showing what is inside an outcome or a sequence, without taking it apart.
//
// An optional, a fallible and a slice are the three shapes almost every diagnostic is written around, and none of
// them can implement `Debug` on its own: what a `T?` looks like depends entirely on what `T` looks like, and native
// forms implement no interface at all. So these are handwritten functions with the bound written out —
// `WriteOptionalDebug<T: Debug>` — which is the shape the language does have. Each instantiation
// resolves its element's `WriteDebug` at compile time; nothing is dispatched at run time and nothing is reflected
// over.
//
// The whole point is that they borrow. `??` and `?` consume the optional, because taking a payload out is a transfer
// of ownership. But showing a value is not taking it: a `match` on a borrowed optional binds its payload where it
// lies, and that binding is what these hand to the element's `WriteDebug`.
// So an option holding a move-only value can be written twice and still hold it, which is the only behaviour worth
// having — a diagnostic that consumed what it printed would be a diagnostic nobody could put in front of a value
// they still needed.
//
// The specification describes the *elements*, not the container. There is no numeric or textual form a sequence
// has of its own for a width or a precision to apply to, so each element is handed the caller's specification
// unchanged and does with it whatever that element type does — `{:x}` over a slice of integers writes each one in
// hexadecimal. The brackets, the commas and the case names are punctuation and are never padded, so a specification
// that an element ignores changes nothing at all.
//
// Sequential containers are in `Rux/Collections`, which borrows a run of elements out of a vector, an array or a
// deque and calls `WriteSliceDebug` with it. Nothing here knows those types exist.

/// Writes a borrowed optional as `Some(value)` or `none`.
///
/// The optional is borrowed and its payload with it, so this can be called on an optional holding a value that
/// cannot be copied, as many times as wanted, and the optional still holds it afterwards.
///
/// `spec` is handed to the payload's own `WriteDebug` and describes it rather than the wrapper.
///
/// # Failures
/// Reports whatever the writer or the payload's implementation reported, stopping at the first refusal, so a
/// destination that has already refused something is not written to again.
/// @typeParam T the payload type, which must know how to show itself
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param value the optional to show, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to show the payload under
/// @returns success when the whole rendering was accepted
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/write-optional-debug
pub func WriteOptionalDebug<T: Debug>(writer: &var TextWriter, value: &T?,
                                    spec: FormatSpec) -> ! FormatError {
    match value {
        .Some(payload) => {
            WriteBytes(writer, "Some(")?;
            payload.WriteDebug(writer, spec)?;
            return WriteAscii(writer, c8')');
        },
        none => {
            return WriteBytes(writer, "none");
        }
    }
}

/// Writes a borrowed fallible as `Success(value)` or `Failure(reason)`.
///
/// Both payloads are borrowed, so neither channel is consumed by being shown. The names are the native channel
/// names, so what is written names the case a reader would branch on.
///
/// `spec` is handed to whichever payload is present and describes it rather than the wrapper.
///
/// # Failures
/// Reports whatever the writer or the payload's implementation reported, stopping at the first refusal.
/// @typeParam T the success payload type, which must know how to show itself
/// @typeParam E the failure payload type, which must know how to show itself
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param value the fallible to show, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to show the payload under
/// @returns success when the whole rendering was accepted
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/write-fallible-debug
pub func WriteFallibleDebug<T: Debug, E: Debug>(writer: &var TextWriter, value: &(T ! E),
                                                spec: FormatSpec) -> ! FormatError {
    match value {
        .Success(payload) => {
            WriteBytes(writer, "Success(")?;
            payload.WriteDebug(writer, spec)?;
            return WriteAscii(writer, c8')');
        },
        .Failure(reason) => {
            WriteBytes(writer, "Failure(")?;
            reason.WriteDebug(writer, spec)?;
            return WriteAscii(writer, c8')');
        }
    }
}

/// Writes a borrowed run of elements as `[first, second, third]`.
///
/// An empty run is `[]`, which is visible where writing nothing would not be. Elements are separated by a comma and
/// a space, and every element is shown, since a diagnostic that elided part of what it was given would be worse
/// than a long one.
///
/// The run is borrowed and each element is shown through a borrow of it, so a slice of a move-only element type is
/// shown without disturbing a single one of them.
///
/// `spec` is handed to each element unchanged and means whatever that element type makes of it. The brackets and
/// commas are punctuation and are never padded.
///
/// # Failures
/// Reports whatever the writer or an element's implementation reported, stopping at the first refusal rather than
/// continuing through the rest of the run.
/// @typeParam T the element type, which must know how to show itself
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param items the run of elements to show, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to show each element under
/// @returns success when the whole rendering was accepted
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/write-slice-debug
pub func WriteSliceDebug<T: Debug>(writer: &var TextWriter, items: T[..],
                                   spec: FormatSpec) -> ! FormatError {
    WriteAscii(writer, c8'[')?;
    var index: uint = 0;
    while index < items.length {
        if index != 0 {
            WriteBytes(writer, ", ")?;
        }
        items.data[index].WriteDebug(writer, spec)?;
        index = index + 1;
    }
    return WriteAscii(writer, c8']');
}
